At 2:37 PM 12/6/94, John M. Bozeman wrote:
>Complete Laissez faire simply suggests that there are no rules
>one way or the other;

Do you mean there are no rules at all, or no rules about slavery?

Laissez faire can't work without property rights, which are rules of a
sort. The distinction between laissez faire and the alternative, to someone
who wishes to take the position seriously, is a question of what kind of
rules are in effect.

As far as slavery goes, I suppose you could call a system of property
rights that included the institution of slavery (as in the pre-civil war
south) laissez faire. This seems to stretch it a bit, since that phrase is
most often associated with classical liberalism, which included
anti-slavery norms. I could say something similar about democracy, eh?

>I don't see why it would not be tolerated
>if all one cares about is the end product.

I'm not sure which end product you're talking about, but in any case that
seems like a very big if.
