From: davisd@taipei.ee.washington.edu
Date: Tue, 06 Dec 94 15:02:43 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-237] The symbiosis of the state and the criminal class

In response to the thread, why Russia is not an anarchy:

Among other reasons, an important aspect I rarely see discussed is the
monopoly power of ruling by the state forces all competition
underground, out of the light of day.

While the competition to the State is powerful, it will largely remain
confined to criminal types because the vast majority, who are peace
loving non violent types, cannot publicly organize to oppose the
violence of the criminal types. The State will prevent them. 

The peaceful types tend to want to avoid violence, and rely on their
massed power to combat breakers of the peace. Force in the name of
justice has large positive externalities; the enforcers gain no more
than a tiny increment in increased peace for risking their lives.
Their power requires open, public visibility. The criminal types rely
on a small band who use violence for great private profit, thus
justifying their risk.

Anywhere that peaceful types can't openly organize and use force,
expect criminals to flourish. Such is the symbiotic relationship
between the criminal class and the government class; the State
protects the criminal class against destruction from a massed public,
and the criminals produce public fear which convinces the public to
empower the state, which only further limits public power, making the
criminals more secure.

